<title>UK: Hot dry English weather ideal for sugar beet.</title>
<headline>Hot dry English weather ideal for sugar beet.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-08-18</dateline>
<text>
<p>Hot dry weather over most of England in recent days has been ideal for the sugar beet crop, sector experts said.</p>
<p>&quot;This weather is first rate for sugar,&quot; a sugar crop expert at the agricultural consultancy ADAS said.</p>
<p>He was bullish on crop prospects. &quot;I'd be very surprised if it isn't outstanding.&quot;</p>
<p>Hot dry weather was good for sugar content, he explained. &quot;If the hot dry weather continues, it'll really pump in the sugar.&quot;</p>
<p>The second British beet test, published August 12, showed 77 grams of sugar per root, compared with 57 last year. At the time, British sugar called prospects for this beet crop good, with a better than average national crop indicated, &quot;if these conditions continue.&quot;</p>
<p>--Christopher Lyddon, London newsroom +44 171 542 7928</p>
